<h2 id="free-certificate-maker-vs-digital-credential-management-platform">Free Certificate Maker vs Digital Credential Management Platform</h2>

<p>These terms are sometimes used interchangeably, but they describe different levels of capability.</p>

<h3 id="a-free-certificate-maker">A free certificate maker</h3>

<p>Typically focuses on:</p>

<p>Design → Generate → Download</p>

<p>This can be sufficient for simple use cases.</p>

<h3 id="a-digital-credential-platform">A digital credential platform</h3>

<p>Can involve:</p>

<p>Create → Issue → Verify → Manage → Share → Analyse</p>

<h3 id="institutional-digital-credential-infrastructure">Institutional digital credential infrastructure</h3>

<p>Can extend further:</p>

<p>Achievement → Credential → Standards → Verification → Skills → Learner Record → Workforce Intelligence → Opportunity</p>

<p>For universities, understanding this distinction is important before selecting a platform.</p>

<h2 id="where-certifyme-fits">Where CertifyMe Fits</h2>

<p>CertifyMe approaches digital credentials as digital credential infrastructure for institutions, rather than treating credentialing simply as certificate generation.</p>

<p>The platform’s model connects six layers:</p>

<h3 id="01--presentation--access">01 — Presentation &amp; Access</h3>

<p>Make achievements visible, branded, accessible and shareable through credential design, credential pages, unique URLs and QR access.</p>

<h3 id="02--standards--interoperability">02 — Standards &amp; Interoperability</h3>

<p>Use recognised standards such as Open Badges 3.0 and W3C Verifiable Credentials to support interoperability.</p>

<h3 id="03--verification--trust">03 — Verification &amp; Trust</h3>

<p>Establish the issuer, recipient and integrity of the credential using mechanisms designed for verifiable achievement.</p>

<h3 id="04--skill-taxonomy-alignment">04 — Skill Taxonomy Alignment</h3>

<p>Connect achievements to structured skill models, including recognised taxonomies and institution-defined skill frameworks where applicable.</p>

<h3 id="05--comprehensive-learner-record">05 — Comprehensive Learner Record</h3>

<p>Bring degrees, credentials, skills, competencies, projects, experiences and other achievements into a connected learner record.</p>

<h3 id="06--workforce-intelligence--opportunity">06 — Workforce Intelligence &amp; Opportunity</h3>

<p>Use verified skills and learner records to understand occupations, jobs, employers, locations, workforce demand and relevant opportunities.</p>

<p>The resulting institutional journey is:</p>

<p>Achievement<br />↓<br />Credential<br />↓<br />Standards<br />↓<br />Verification<br />↓<br />Skills<br />↓<br />Learner Record<br />↓<br />Workforce Intelligence<br />↓<br />Opportunity</p>

<p>This is the distinction between simply issuing digital certificates and building a broader institutional credential infrastructure.</p>

<h2 id="when-should-a-university-move-beyond-a-free-platform">When Should a University Move Beyond a Free Platform?</h2>

<p>There is no universal threshold.</p>

<p>A university should consider moving beyond a free or limited solution when its requirements begin to include:</p>

<ul>
  <li>Multiple departments or programmes</li>
  <li>Larger credential volumes</li>
  <li>Institutional governance</li>
  <li>Automated issuance</li>
  <li>System integrations</li>
  <li>Standards interoperability</li>
  <li>Formal verification</li>
  <li>Analytics</li>
  <li>Skill mapping</li>
  <li>Learner records</li>
  <li>Long-term credential management</li>
  <li>Workforce and career outcomes</li>
</ul>

<p>The decision should therefore be based on institutional requirements rather than the number of free credentials available.</p>

<p>A free tier may be an excellent way to validate an idea.</p>

<p>It may not necessarily be the right foundation for an institution-wide credential strategy.</p>

<h2 id="questions-to-ask-before-choosing-a-digital-credential-platform">Questions to Ask Before Choosing a Digital Credential Platform</h2>

<p>Before selecting a platform, universities should ask:</p>

<h3 id="is-it-standards-based">Is it standards-based?</h3>

<p>Can the credentials operate beyond the platform?</p>

<h3 id="can-employers-and-other-institutions-verify-credentials">Can employers and other institutions verify credentials?</h3>

<p>Verification should be simple and trustworthy.</p>

<h3 id="can-it-integrate-with-our-existing-systems">Can it integrate with our existing systems?</h3>

<p>The credential workflow should fit the institution’s technology environment.</p>

<h3 id="can-different-departments-operate-under-appropriate-governance">Can different departments operate under appropriate governance?</h3>

<p>Institution-wide credentialing requires administrative control.</p>

<h3 id="can-achievements-be-connected-to-skills">Can achievements be connected to skills?</h3>

<p>This helps move beyond credential issuance toward structured learner outcomes.</p>

<h3 id="can-multiple-achievements-become-a-learner-record">Can multiple achievements become a learner record?</h3>

<p>This is important for institutions building a longer-term learner identity.</p>

<h3 id="can-the-platform-support-workforce-context">Can the platform support workforce context?</h3>

<p>For institutions focused on employability, skills and learner outcomes, this can become increasingly valuable.</p>

