<h2 id="final-takeaway">Final Takeaway</h2>

<p>Free digital credential software can be a good starting point—but the right starting point depends on the problem you are trying to solve.</p>

<p>For a single workshop or small event, a simple free tool may be enough.</p>

<p>For a university building a long-term digital credential strategy, the evaluation needs to go further.</p>

<p>The important questions become:</p>

<ul>
  <li>Can credentials be trusted?</li>
  <li>Can they work across systems?</li>
  <li>Can the institution govern them?</li>
  <li>Can achievements connect to skills?</li>
  <li>Can those achievements contribute to a comprehensive learner record?</li>
  <li>Can verified skills eventually connect learners with workforce opportunities?</li>
</ul>

<p>That is where digital credentialing evolves from certificate creation to institutional digital credential infrastructure.</p>

